# E4 capture decision: one dense-frontier traversal wave

21 October 2026. Decision from the single authenticated, named-Wasm E4 capture; no further timing was run. Candidate is a **contiguous full-frontier adapter inside the current retained gather**, preserving the one semantic authority, one packed/SoA workspace and one Codec/ordered-plan executor. It is a bounded potential improvement, not a promise of whole-gap parity. A real smaller win with no seven-family regression is worth retaining and can combine with later justified changes.

## Evidence and limits

Frozen archive SHA-256 `9b1ad97a03f5af8eaa0613e5bdaab601edf7fccf69fdeacc6059759191b1fb18`; named Wasm SHA-256 `806066af37452bbf5781955c8bb6f2083997cc68fd0e5eae485612ea5b608826`; executable sections match shipping SHA-256 `d14d1fc68071da0127addec4dcb044998a7da4b16b32fa9c3a84eb7c70cc1018`. Root's preflight checked all 666 frozen inputs, classified four current differences (two Labs harness helpers, lockfile, workspace file), matched 12 actual Cargo locks, and matched all 947 archive members. The archive is authenticated E4 product; this does not make the historical build environment identical to the current consumer. The ordinary named workflow does not retain hashes of all 947 installed members or the isolated resolved peer tree. The profile manifest authenticates its Wasm override and archive, not final acceptance.

Raw profile SHA-256 `2df04d4827cb1b80246c618989109654348f288a8eb2d7edb15f00a83624dadb`; exact 1,024 distinct labels, 10 warmups, 80 callbacks/81,920 timed setters, zero timed reads, 80 scene publications, one 9,216-glyph draw. `updateBulk` node 6 has disjoint child `set text` node 7 and `updateMatrixWorld` node 38, verified from raw nodes/timedeltas. Exclusive samples: setters 469 samples/703.581 ms; publication 189/282.587 ms; detached 41/93.374 ms, 699 samples/1,079.542 ms total. Profiled callback p50/p95 12.981/14.663 ms is diagnostic, not an unprofiled parity measure. The stock “other-wasm” stage is heterogeneous; named frames, not that bucket label, ground the following choice.

Setter's named hotspots include HarfRust `apply_forward` 41.877 ms and pair-positioning 30.165+19.624 ms, positioning 24.124 ms, `prepare_clusters` 24.001 ms, flow 15.081 ms, and TS `_updateText` 21.000 ms/`#prepareTextCandidate` 14.999 ms across the 80 callbacks. Some setter transport/preflight exists (`validate_planner_inputs` 10.459 ms, `frame-wire` frames), but source shows no demonstrated duplicate semantic pass or clearly removable per-set transaction. The accepted synchronous contract requires each distinct label to acquire a prepared semantic state. Thus the larger setter phase alone is **not** a license for a request-fusion patch.

Publication samples identify current general-range machinery on the exact dense fixture: `retained_rope::update_ordered_node<...OwnerRanges>` 21.084 ms/14 samples, `OrderedRanges<OwnerRanges>::advance_to` 10.542 ms/7, and `planner_gather_owners` iterator 9.000 ms/6. `update_fields` 21.043 ms and Codec `execute_program` 18.126 ms are shared required work and remain. `prepare_placement_slots` 12.124 ms and ordered-plan/host frames also remain. The 21.084 ms rope node includes real traversal and leaf reconstruction; only the owner-range/admission part is a plausible deletion. The most directly removable named frame is the 10.542 ms `OwnerRanges` advance, about 0.132 ms per profiled callback if every sample were saved; this is **not** an elapsed forecast. E2 native work counts describe a similar shape but are not E4 timing. A dense adapter must beat its own admission cost; no arbitrary 5% or entire-gap threshold applies.

## Medium implementation boundary and proof gate

**Before changing the executor**, preserve/run a cold independent E4 oracle for the exact alpha/bravo 1,024-label final state and a small structural set: sparse one-owner edit, count change, recordless/selected transition, decoration, reorder, multi-font selection, rejected renderer publication and retry. Compare warm against independently constructed cold semantic text, measurement, glyph/cluster/flow/positioned columns (normalizing identity fields only where the contract permits), independently copied glyphs, packed physical bytes, patch ranges, bindings/batch order, draw topology and accepted-generation behavior. Existing Rust `state.rs` cold-shape helpers and retained-gather tests, package tests and renderer recovery fixtures are starting points; the profile's post-timing text/positive-measure/availability checks do not supply this physical cold oracle. If no existing named test covers a required comparison, add a narrowly scoped test first and obtain a green E4 baseline. Do not weaken public setters, prepared queries, split, failure/order behavior or the SIMD/SoA path.

Then prove from committed source/tests that the proposed mode is a special traversal of the **same** `append_retained_stream` and `update_retained_source` pipeline, with identical error order and `RopeUpdate::Stop` → `replace_planner_gather` fallback. `RetainedRope::update_ordered_ranges` is transactional: changed leaves/branches are reconstructed; it is **not** a mutable in-place rope. Packed aligned fields/selected glyph slots already receive indexed writes. Current `prepare_update_inner` clears gather cache before the gather and invalidates the root after a preparation error; verify a partial lane write cannot be observed through the last accepted renderer generation or borrowed plan, and that next reset/rebuild clears it. A late error or Stop must not publish a half-updated plan. If this cannot be shown without a second full packed snapshot or changing the error fence, stop this wave and report the blocker.

Use `state.rs::prepare_retained_order(Gather)` as the existing exact owner validator. Admit the dense adapter only when the retained cache key matches, no checkpoint/lifecycle/order/decorated topology change applies, every active ordered owner is present exactly once on the dirty frontier, each new positioned source count equals its prior gather range, those ranges cover one contiguous source interval, **and each owner's `unpublished_source_intervals()` is either absent/full or exactly covers its source range without a clean gap**. The last condition matters: all owners dirty does not imply all their glyph rows dirty, and a single full range must not silently visit clean source intervals. `order_sort_scratch` is already sorted/deduplicated and each entry validated against `active_order`; its length equaling `active_order.len()` proves full coverage if the existing uniqueness invariant holds. Prove that implication from source or check it in a single O(N) ordered pass; do **not** add a second O(G) selection/record preflight. The existing `update_retained_source` detects changed selection, identity and storage topology, and its Stop fallback remains authoritative. These conditions are an invariant-based branch, not a benchmark-percentage heuristic.

Thread a private `full_frontier` mode into `append_planner_gather`/`codec_gather.rs::append_retained_stream`. In this mode give `update_ordered_ranges` one contiguous source range and advance the already ordered owner cursor at owner boundaries; do not construct 1,024 disjoint/gap-oriented `OwnerRanges` intervals or ask the old rope clone for clean-gap prefix summaries. Reuse the same per-source `update_retained_source`, `update_fields`, `GatherSource` selection, aligned lanes, output intervals, Codec scalar/SIMD interpreter and `OrderedPlanCompiler`; do not add per-record locators, a second gathered buffer, a shadow Unicode parser, new ABI or another executor. Keep `OwnerRanges`, gap prefix summaries and replacement logic for sparse/mixed/count-changing work. E3's reset/repopulate and E5/E6's locator/view representations are not part of this wave. The dense branch still does O(N + G + GF) work plus transactional changed-leaf copies; it deletes only general-range selection overhead when every owner is touched. If an O(N) admission pass costs more than saved owner-range work, discard it.

Targeted verification before the full matrix: show the warm dense lane enters the new branch; record exact source/selected rows, range iterator calls, changed rope leaves/copies, packed field reads/writes, allocation/reallocation/live bytes, Codec output bytes and fallback counts. Expect no change in HarfRust shape count, required packed output, SIMD executable path or publication result. For sparse, decoration, count/selection-change and rejection histories, require old route or identical fallback and cold oracle. Use the named package test/static workflows; use an unprofiled same-machine 512/1,024 dense check plus one sparse negative control only after cold gates. Keep the patch only on a repeatable actual complete-operation improvement with no meaningful negative-control regression and no size/memory/ABI surprise. A smaller real win can be retained even if parity remains open; a no-op or loss is deleted rather than hidden behind an aggregate.

For acceptance, freeze one shipping archive and run the fixed seven-family single-call matrix against main **and** published 0.1.0 on the same machine, E2 continuity, full p50/p95/tail/exclusions/drift, package/peer/ABI/size and engine/whole-app memory, downstream CPU renderer recovery and browser pixels. Do not claim parity from the profile or this one wave. Preserve the held stack and no merge/release/tag/demo merge.

Primary evidence: `/Volumes/Depot/Developer/glyph/.cache/held-e4-attribution-20261021/evidence/e4-1024-named-profile/{cpu-profile.json,summary.json,manifest.json,ancestry.json,ancestry-proof.json,exclusive.md}` and neighboring root authentication receipts; committed source `packages/glyph/rust/shaper/src/engine/{state.rs,codec_gather.rs,retained_rope.rs,ordered_plan.rs}`, `packages/glyph/src/internal/{render-planner.ts,handle-state.ts}`. This decision supersedes the earlier report's broader *new same-count preflight* idea: the narrower direct-range branch reuses the existing retained helper and fallback, avoiding a duplicate selection walk.
